{"id": "section-body-13664", "title": "Chapter Fourteen", "chapterId": "chapter-fourteen", "blocks": [{"id": "body-13664", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading3", "text": "CHAPTER FOURTEEN", "runs": [{"text": "CHAPTER"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "FOURTEEN"}], "level": 3}, {"id": "body-13665", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading3", "text": "EYE DISEASES AND REFRACTIVE ERRORS", "runs": [{"text": "EYE"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "DISEASES"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "AND"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "REFRACTIVE"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "ERRORS"}], "level": 3}, {"id": "body-13667", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading2", "text": "Major Blinding Eye Diseases", "runs": [{"text": "Major"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Blinding"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Eye"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Diseases"}], "level": 2, "list": {"level": 1, "numberingId": "1313"}}, {"id": "body-13668", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Eye diseases and refractive errors are conditions affecting the eye. Diseases of most important are those leading to blindness, visual impairment (VI) or presenting with pain. Blindness is defined as a presenting visual acuity (VA) of less than 3/60 or central visual field of less than 10º in the better eye. Visual impairment is categorized by WHO onto several categories depends on VA. The definition of blindness/VI in children and infants is the same as that in adults though there are different methods for testing vision in young children until when they are at preschool age when normal visual acuity chart can be used. The common causes of blindness and visual impairment among adults are cataract, glaucoma, corneal blindness, diabetic retinopathy, uncorrected refractive errors. There are other eye diseases that do not lead into blindness or visual impairment, these are also addressed in this section. Given the asymptomatic nature of most eye diseases, it is advised that patients presenting to eye clinic with whatever complaint must have basic eye examination of both eyes which entails (VA-Plain and PH, IOP, fundoscopy and pupil exam) as a minimum.", "runs": [{"text": "Eye"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "diseases"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "refractive"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "errors"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "conditions"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "affecting"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "eye."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Diseases"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "most"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "important"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "those"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "leading"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "to"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "blindness, visual"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "impairment"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(VI)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "presenting"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "pain."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Blindness"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "defined"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "as"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "presenting"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "visual"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "acuity"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(VA)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "less"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "than"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "3/60"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "central visual field of less than 10º in the better eye. Visual impairment is categorized by WHO onto several categories depends on VA. The definition of blindness/VI in children and infants is the same as that in adults though there are different methods for testing"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "vision"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "in"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "young"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "children"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "until"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "when"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "they"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "are"}, 